Interview Questions
Q1. Why do we need a Service layer?

To keep business logic separate from the Controller and database logic. This follows the Separation of Concerns principle.

Q2. Can the Controller directly call the Repository?

Technically yes, but it is not recommended. It mixes request handling with business logic, making the code difficult to maintain and test.

Q3. What kind of logic belongs in the Service layer?

Examples:

Validate business rules.
Check if email already exists.
Call multiple repositories.
Send email after registration.
Apply discounts.
Perform calculations.
Q4. What does @Service do?

It marks the class as a Service component and registers it as a Spring Bean.

Q5. What is @Autowired?

@Autowired tells Spring to inject the required dependency automatically from the IoC container.

Q6. What is Dependency Injection?

Dependency Injection is the process where Spring creates the required object and injects it into another class instead of us creating it manually.

Q7. Why is Dependency Injection better than using new?

Without DI:

UserRepository repository = new UserRepository();

Problems:

Tight coupling.
Hard to test.
Spring cannot manage the object.

With DI:

@Autowired
private UserRepository repository;

Benefits:

Loose coupling.
Easy testing.
Spring manages the object lifecycle.
⭐ Common Follow-up
Constructor Injection vs Field Injection?

Field Injection

@Autowired
private UserRepository repository;

Easy but not recommended for production.

Constructor Injection

private final UserRepository repository;

public UserService(UserRepository repository) {
    this.repository = repository;
}

Preferred because:

Better for testing.
Supports immutable dependencies (final).
Makes dependencies explicit.


